<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mosul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mosul is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, opposite the ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>. In the twelfth century it was a base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a>, in whose service <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> and his family rose to power. Its politics were thus closely linked to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosul.jpg" alt="Mosul" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copyright © 2013 Younus Alhamdani · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B5%D9%84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mosul stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, and the ruins of the ancient Assyrian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> lie on its east side. The Arabic name Mawsil means linking point, or Junction City, and its strategic and central location made it a hub of trade and travel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> still call the whole city Nineveh. Xenophon mentions a small Assyrian town called Mépsila on the Tigris in 401 BCE, though it may be safer to identify it with Iski Mosul, about 30 kilometres north, where the Sasanian centre of Budh-Ardhashir was built six centuries later. The area was part of Assyria from as early as the 25th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Muslim conquests of the mid-7th century, Arab, Kurdish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> gradually settled in the region. In the late 9th century the Turkic dynasts Ishaq ibn Kundaj and his son Muhammad seized Mosul, and in 893 it came again under direct Abbasid control. In the early 10th century the Arab Hamdanid dynasty took it. Under Abdallah ibn Hamdan and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a> the Hamdanids expanded their control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, first as Abbasid governors and later as de facto independent rulers. A century later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylid dynasty</a> supplanted them. In 969 the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> called it a fine town with excellent markets.</p><p>In the 13th century Mosul was a wealthy capital, first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a> and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a>, and made luxury brass vessels inlaid with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Of all medieval Islamic artefacts, Mosul brasswork has the most inscriptions. The Andalusian geographer Ibn Sa'id, who passed through about 1250, is the only contemporary source to mention the industry. Sultan Suleiman captured Mosul from Safavid Persia in 1538 and the city was governed by a pasha. It was famed for its walls, with seven gates, a hospital and a covered market. British forces occupied it in October 1918. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> contested the mandate, and in 1926 an agreement brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s possession.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mosul is the capital of Nineveh Governorate in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Its people are mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a> and Shabaks, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> is the largest religion, and there are many Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a>. The local dialect is North Mesopotamian Arabic, called Maslawi. The city has historic mosques, Christian sites and synagogues, and the University of Mosul. The metropolitan area spreads over both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mosul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He sent his brother Turanshah to conquer Yemen in 1174, which secured the Red Sea route, took Aleppo in 1183 and received the submission of <b>Mosul</b> in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The brothers and their heirs fought on many fronts: against the Hamdanids of <b>Mosul</b> in the north, the Samanids of Khurasan and the Ziyarids of Gurgan in the east, and rival dynasties in Kerman and Oman, which Adud al-Dawla subdued in the late 960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling family descended from the Baharlu of the Yıwa tribe of the Oghuz Turks, who by the fourteenth century held lands north of Lake Van and around <b>Mosul</b> in Upper Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1127, after the murder of Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi, atabeg of <b>Mosul</b>, the Seljuks named Zengi, son of Aq Sunqur al-Hajib, their former governor of Aleppo, as the new atabeg; he had already been a successful Seljuk general and governor in Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the 12th century Yazidis appear in the sources as Dāsinī, after the region of Dāsin northeast of <b>Mosul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Heavily outnumbered, Saladin took the hills by the Orontes and surrounded the attacking armies of <b>Mosul</b> and Aleppo.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
